-"Let $U\\subseteq\\mathbb R^m$ be open, let $a\\in U$, and let $f:U\\to\\mathbb R^n$. The map $f$ is **totally differentiable at $a$** when there is a linear map $L:\\mathbb R^m\\to\\mathbb R^n$ ([[def-euclidean-linear-map]]) such that\n\n$$\\lim_{h\\to0}\\frac{\\|f(a+h)-f(a)-Lh\\|_2}{\\|h\\|_2}=0,$$\n\nwhere the quotient is considered for $h\\ne0$ with $a+h\\in U$. The map $L$, when it exists, is denoted $Df(a)$ and called the **total derivative**. Equivalently, $f(a+h)=f(a)+Df(a)h+r(h)$ with $\\|r(h)\\|_2/\\|h\\|_2\\to0$."+"Let $m,n\\in\\mathbb N$, let $U\\subseteq\\mathbb R^m$ be open in the metric induced by its Euclidean norm, let $a\\in U$, and let $f:U\\to\\mathbb R^n$. The map $f$ is **totally differentiable at $a$** when there is a linear map $L:\\mathbb R^m\\to\\mathbb R^n$ ([[def-euclidean-linear-map]]) such that for every $\\varepsilon>0$ there is $\\delta>0$ for which\n\n$$\\|f(a+h)-f(a)-Lh\\|_2\\le\\varepsilon\\|h\\|_2\\qquad\\text{whenever }a+h\\in U\\text{ and }\\|h\\|_2<\\delta.$$\n\nThe map $L$, when it exists, is denoted $Df(a)$ and called the **total derivative**. Equivalently, $f(a+h)=f(a)+Df(a)h+r(h)$ with the displayed remainder estimate for every $\\varepsilon>0$. At $h=0$ the remainder is zero because $L0=0$."
